 "notes": [
  "On 1h, ADX(14) crossing up through 25 gave 2,810 entries in 2,344 separate moves. Twenty candles later they were 0.09% ahead of random entries, inside a luck band of -0.12% to 0.12%. SOL looked best at 0.25%, but its band reached 0.26%.",
  "Staying above 25 on 1h enters far more often than the cross, because it fires on every candle the level holds. Those 80,667 entries bunch into 1,477 moves, fewer than the cross gives. The result is the same: 0.02% against random, inside a band of -0.07% to 0.05%.",
  "A very strong trend reading did not help either. Above 40, longs were 0.38% ahead of random on 4h, against a band of -0.63% to 0.64%. On daily they were 1.59% ahead after 20 days, against a band of -4.86% to 5.67%. ADX rises in sharp sell-offs as well as in rallies, so a high value says nothing about direction.",
  "Quiet markets did not lead to a move up. On 4h, ADX(14) crossing down through 20 gave -0.26% against random, inside a band of -0.51% to 0.48%. On 1h, the time spent below 20 came out at -0.03%.",
  "Daily results are thin. ADX(14) above 25 on daily gives 64 moves pooled, and on BTC alone only 21, under the 30 this page needs before it judges a coin. The pooled gap of 0.23% sits inside a band of -2.23% to 1.43%. Do not read the single-coin daily numbers as a signal.",
  "Because ADX carries no direction, it only works next to a condition that sets the side, such as a moving average or price above a recent high. Use it to decide when a long or short bot may open, then let the DCA orders and take profit handle the exit. If your backtest shows no difference with the ADX filter on and off, leave it out. That matches what this data shows on BTC, ETH and SOL."
 ],
